# @cover src/compiler/20.hir/hir_lowering/items.spl 5%
"""
# hir_lowering `items.*` surface completeness

**Category:** Compiler / HIR

`items.spl` re-exports every `_Items/*` extension module, so a consumer that
imports `compiler.hir.hir_lowering.items.*` gets the item-lowering methods on
`HirLowering`. It did NOT re-export `statements.spl`, so the statement-lowering
impl block was never in the compilation unit and any such consumer failed with
`method lower_hir_stmt_multi not found on type HirLowering`. That is a real gap
rather than a spec inconvenience: `_Expressions/block_and_asm_lowering.spl`
calls `self.lower_hir_stmt_multi(...)` from inside the expression surface that
every `_Items` module imports, so the item surface genuinely depends on it.

Two existing specs had to add a local `use compiler.hir.hir_lowering.statements.*`
purely as a workaround. This spec deliberately imports ONLY the `items.*`
harness so it fails before the re-export is added and passes after.
"""

use std.spec
use compiler.frontend.parser_types.*
use compiler.frontend.parser_types_expr.*
use compiler.common.diagnostics.span.{Span}
use compiler.hir.hir_lowering.types.{HirLowering}
use compiler.hir.hir_lowering.items.*

describe "the items.* harness carries the statement-lowering surface":
    it "lower_hir_stmt_multi is callable on HirLowering with only items.* imported":
        var lowering = HirLowering.with_filename("src/items_surface_fixture.spl")
        val sp = Span(0, 0, 1, 1)
        val stmt = Stmt(kind: StmtKind.Expr(Expr(kind: ExprKind.IntLit(7), span: sp)), span: sp)
        val lowered = lowering.lower_hir_stmt_multi(stmt)
        expect(lowered.len()).to_equal(1)
